n files  to make everything flow through  seamlessly devtools can automatically  generate  our our documentation from the  our oxygen comments that we have already  wrote  many steps back in this process and test  that provides a really friendly  interface  for doing unit testing which quite  honestly objectively if we want to trust  the outcome of any analysis we probably  even could have thought about doing  many steps earlier in this process  and beyond all of those absolute  requirements  r even goes above and beyond what we  need and there also exists package down  which can similarly reuse all of those  great assets you made  throughout your project and throughout  the art our  markdown driven development process and  create a very user-friendly  attractive polished website to help  people learn about  and use your analytical tool  so now we've created kind of the  spectrum of possible outputs  and possible analytical outcomes of our  markdown driven development process  i think all of us are probably very well  aware what incremental benefits the  package provides  it's of course one of the most formal  distribution mechanisms  especially if you have access to a  repository a cran  or cran-like repository to share your  work  or and it also adds benefits  because it provides a very formal and  familiar way  for people to learn about and engage  with your tool pretty much any our user  is used to working within our package  that said kind of the trade-off again  that exists here  is that at this point we've abstracted  very far away  from our initial problem and if simply  recreating your work  is you think that going to be the  primary goal of future users  then maybe we've gone a bit too far  so in summary no matter which path you  choose  our marked your original our markdown  analysis can be a great starting point  for a very wide variety of data products  and by leveraging all the work you did  in conducting your initial analysis  you're much closer than 